I am a beginner learning TypeScript by building a real app over 14 days, spending about 1 hour per day.

## App: Perfume of the Day Picker

A web app where I can:
- Add perfumes to my collection (name, brand, scent notes, season, occasion tags like casual/office/date night, rating, image optional)
- Browse and manage my collection (edit, delete, view details)
- Get a "Perfume of the Day" suggestion based on occasion or mood I select
- Filter/search my collection by season, occasion, or scent notes

## Tech Stack
- TypeScript (strict, no `any` ever)
- React + Next.js (App Router)
- Supabase (database + auth)
- Tailwind CSS for styling
- Vercel for deployment

## My Learning Goals
- Master TypeScript: strict types, interfaces, enums, generics
- Build typed React components with props, state, events, forms
- Connect frontend to Supabase (auth + database, typed queries)
- Be able to independently build full-stack apps after this

## Rules for the 14-Day Plan
- One task per day, each task builds on the previous day's work
- Every day must include:
  - What to build (specific feature or piece of the app)
  - What TypeScript concept it teaches
  - "Done When" criteria so I know I finished
- Start from `npx create-next-app` on Day 1 and end with a deployed app on Day 14
- Group into phases:
  - Days 1-3: Project setup + TypeScript foundations (types, interfaces, enums applied in the app)
  - Days 4-6: React UI (components, state, forms for adding/managing perfumes)
  - Days 7-9: Supabase integration (database, auth, CRUD operations)
  - Days 10-12: Core feature (suggestion engine, filtering, search)
  - Days 13-14: Polish and deploy (loading states, error handling, Vercel deployment)
- I write ALL code myself. Do not write the solution. Only give me the task and guidance.
- Each day's task should be completable in roughly 1 hour
- No libraries beyond the tech stack listed above

